public with sharing class ContactPhoneCleanup {

    public static Map<Id, String> normalizePhones(List<Id> contactIds) {
        Map<Id, String> errors = new Map<Id, String>();

        if (contactIds == null || contactIds.isEmpty()) {
            return errors;
        }

        List<Contact> contacts = [
            SELECT Id, Phone
            FROM Contact
            WHERE Id IN :contactIds
        ];

        List<Contact> toUpdate = new List<Contact>();
        for (Contact c : contacts) {
            if (String.isBlank(c.Phone)) {
                continue;
            }
            c.Phone = c.Phone.replaceAll('[^0-9+]', '');
            toUpdate.add(c);
        }

        if (toUpdate.isEmpty()) {
            return errors;
        }

        Database.SaveResult[] results = Database.update(toUpdate, false);

        for (Integer i = 0; i < results.size(); i++) {
            Database.SaveResult sr = results[i];
            if (!sr.isSuccess()) {
                errors.put(toUpdate[i].Id, sr.getErrors()[0].getMessage());
            }
        }

        return errors;
    }
}
```

Notes on what I added versus the bare description:

- **I query the contacts from the Ids** rather than assuming you already have the records. The method signature only gives `List<Id>`, so a SOQL query is required to read and update `Phone`. It's a single bulk query, safe for 200 records.
- **`String.isBlank` instead of `!= null`** so a contact whose phone is an empty/whitespace string is also "left out" and not pushed through an empty update.
- **`replaceAll('[^0-9+]', '')`** strips everything that isn't a digit or `+`, exactly matching your example. (If a stray `+` can appear mid-string, this keeps it — the spec says keep `+`, so I don't special-case leading-only.)
- Everything else follows your approach: one `Database.update(..., false)`, a loop over the results, and a map of failed Id → first error message (empty when all succeed). I map results back by index, which is reliable because `Database.update` returns results in the same order as the input list.

