public with sharing class CasePriorityHandler {

    /**
     * Sets Priority to 'High' for any new Case whose Account has Rating = 'Hot'.
     * All other cases keep the Priority they were created with, including
     * cases that have no Account.
     *
     * Note for maintainers: the Account Rating is looked up with a single
     * bulk query (not one query per case). This is required so the ERP
     * integration, which inserts up to 200 cases at once, does not blow the
     * Apex limit of 100 SOQL queries per transaction.
     */
    public static void setPriorityFromAccountRating(List<Case> newCases) {

        // 1. Collect the Account Ids we actually need to look up.
        Set<Id> accountIds = new Set<Id>();
        for (Case c : newCases) {
            if (c.AccountId != null) {
                accountIds.add(c.AccountId);
            }
        }

        // Nothing to do if none of the cases have an account.
        if (accountIds.isEmpty()) {
            return;
        }

        // 2. One query for every account referenced in this batch.
        Map<Id, Account> accountsById = new Map<Id, Account>(
            [SELECT Id, Rating FROM Account WHERE Id IN :accountIds]
        );

        // 3. Promote the priority for cases on a 'Hot' account.
        for (Case c : newCases) {
            if (c.AccountId != null) {
                Account acct = accountsById.get(c.AccountId);
                if (acct != null && acct.Rating == 'Hot') {
                    c.Priority = 'High';
                }
            }
        }
    }
}
```

This keeps the handler simple and readable while staying safe for both the one-at-a-time Email-to-Case path and the 200-at-a-time ERP batches.

I'll make the callouts asynchronously, which is a necessary deviation: Apex cannot do a synchronous HTTP callout from an `after insert` trigger. I use a `Queueable` (allows callouts). To honour "one request per account" while staying within the governor limit of 100 callouts per transaction, each Queueable processes at most 100 accounts (one callout each); with the 200-account marketplace batch this means two Queueables are enqueued.

