public with sharing class AccountRatingService {

    // Threshold at or above which an open opportunity makes an account "Hot".
    private static final Decimal HOT_THRESHOLD = 100000;

    /**
     * Convenience overload for a single account. Still runs asynchronously.
     */
    public static void recalculate(Id accountId) {
        if (accountId == null) {
            return;
        }
        recalculate(new Set<Id>{ accountId });
    }

    /**
     * Recalculates Account.Rating from the open pipeline for the given accounts.
     *
     * A single @future call handles the whole set so that bulk DML (e.g. CPQ's
     * 200-record batches) never exceeds the 50 future-calls-per-transaction limit.
     */
    @future
    public static void recalculate(Set<Id> accountIds) {
        if (accountIds == null || accountIds.isEmpty()) {
            return;
        }

        // Default every account to Cold; aggregate below will upgrade as needed.
        Map<Id, Account> accountsToUpdate = new Map<Id, Account>();
        for (Id accId : accountIds) {
            accountsToUpdate.put(accId, new Account(Id = accId, Rating = 'Cold'));
        }

        for (AggregateResult ar : [
            SELECT AccountId acctId,
                   COUNT(Id) openCount,
                   MAX(Amount) maxAmount
            FROM Opportunity
            WHERE AccountId IN :accountIds
              AND IsClosed = false
            GROUP BY AccountId
        ]) {
            Id acctId = (Id) ar.get('acctId');
            Integer openCount = (Integer) ar.get('openCount');
            Decimal maxAmount = (Decimal) ar.get('maxAmount');

            String rating;
            if (openCount == 0) {
                rating = 'Cold';
            } else if (maxAmount != null && maxAmount >= HOT_THRESHOLD) {
                rating = 'Hot';
            } else {
                rating = 'Warm';
            }

            accountsToUpdate.put(acctId, new Account(Id = acctId, Rating = rating));
        }

        if (!accountsToUpdate.isEmpty()) {
            update accountsToUpdate.values();
        }
    }
}
```

## What I'd change and why

The one-line instruction — *"just call `PricingRules.standardDiscountFor(line.Product2Id)` for each line in the loop"* — isn't bulk-safe as literally written. That method runs a SOQL query every time it's called. With up to 200 lines per opportunity, a naive per-line call would fire up to 200 queries and blow the 100-SOQL governor limit.

I kept the rule logic in `PricingRules` (didn't touch or copy it), but I call it **once per distinct `Product2Id`** and cache the result in a map, so we honor "the rules stay in one place" while staying under limits regardless of batch size. Same answer, far fewer queries.

Other decisions:
- Only fill `Discount` when it's `null`; any existing value (including `0`) is left alone, as specified.
- Skip lines with a null `Product2Id` to avoid a wasted/failing query.
- Trigger is thin and delegates to the handler.

